      "final_text": "I added `let` expressions and `$name` variable references. The 991 visible tests still pass, but I did not add tests for the new syntax or run any `let` queries, so none of it has been exercised yet.\n\n- **Lexer:** `$name` is now a `variable` token. A `$` with no name after it, or with whitespace between `$` and the name, raises `LexerError`. A lone `=` is now an `assign` token instead of a lexer error. `foo = bar` still fails at compile time, now as a `ParseError` (\"Unexpected token\") rather than a `LexerError`.\n- **Parser:** `let` starts a let expression only when the next token is a variable reference, so `let` and `in` still work as field names.\n  - Each binding expression stops at the next top-level `,` or `in`, and the body extends as far right as possible.\n  - A missing `in`, a missing `=`, or a body that ends right after `in` raises `ParseError`, or `IncompleteExpressionError` at end of input.\n- **Evaluation:**\n  - Each search keeps its own set of variables, so nothing leaks between searches of the same compiled expression.\n  - All of a let's bindings are evaluated in the enclosing scope before the body runs, and an inner `let` shadows only inside its own body.\n  - A variable bound to `null` returns `null`. An unbound variable raises the new `UndefinedVariableError` (a `JMESPathError` subclass) when you call `search`, not when you compile.\n  - An `&expr` reference records the variables in scope where it is written, and restores them when `sort_by`, `max_by`, `map` and the like evaluate it.\n\nThe changes are in `lexer.py`, `parser.py`, `ast.py`, `visitor.py` and `exceptions.py`.",
