      "final_text": "I added `jmespath/types.py` and switched `functions.py` and `visitor.py` over to it. The visible suite passes (991 passed, 1 skipped). I didn't write extra tests for the odd inputs the task lists (`Decimal`, `Fraction`, tuples, subclasses of `dict`, `list`, `str` and `int`).\n\n- **`jmespath/types.py`**: it holds `TYPES_MAP` and `REVERSE_TYPES_MAP` (moved unchanged) plus three functions:\n  - `jmespath_type(value)` looks up `type(value).__name__` in `TYPES_MAP` and returns `\"unknown\"` if it isn't there. That is the same lookup the type checker does today.\n  - `is_number(value)` is false for `bool` and otherwise true for any `numbers.Number`.\n  - `is_comparable(value)` is true for a number or a `str`.\n  - It imports only `numbers`, nothing from `jmespath`.\n- **`functions.py`**: it no longer defines the two tables. It imports them from `jmespath.types`, so `from jmespath.functions import TYPES_MAP, REVERSE_TYPES_MAP` still works and gives the same objects.\n- **`Functions._convert_to_jmespath_type`**: I left it as it was. It takes a type name, not a value, and subclasses may override it. The internal calls still go through it.\n- **`visitor.py`**: I removed `_is_comparable` and `_is_actual_number` and replaced their uses with `is_comparable` and `is_number`. I also deleted an unused `num_types` variable in `visit_comparator`.",
